course: dictionary
lesson: 12741
title: Drowsiness: the sleepy feeling
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Drowsiness is a noun, a name for a feeling. It means being sleepy and only half awake, with heavy eyelids. The describing word is drowsy: «Mum felt drowsy after lunch.»
explain: Context gives the clues. «The medicine may cause drowsiness, so do not drive after taking it.» The warning tells you the medicine makes you sleepy, so adults must not drive. Check by swapping in your meaning: «may cause sleepiness» still makes sense.
explain: In stories, drowsiness shows what a character feels. «Her eyelids grew heavy, and a warm drowsiness crept over her beside the crackling fire.» She is drifting off to sleep, and the heavy eyelids are the clue.
fact: Drowsiness is a noun: it names a feeling.
fact: «Drowsiness» means the feeling of being sleepy and only half awake.
fact: Drowsiness is uncountable, so we say «a wave of drowsiness», not «a drowsinesses».
fact: Some medicines «may cause drowsiness», so adults must not drive after taking them.
fact: Heavy eyelids are a common sign of drowsiness.
exercise: «The medicine may cause drowsiness.» What does drowsiness mean? | Feeling hungry | Feeling very wide awake and full of energy | *Feeling sleepy and only half awake | Feeling cold and sick
exercise: «She yawned, her eyelids heavy with drowsiness.» Find and copy the word that names the feeling. | yawned | *drowsiness | eyelids | heavy
exercise: Which sentence shows drowsiness? | «She laughed loudly at the joke.» | «He ran all the way to school.» | «The bright sun made the sand hot.» | *«His eyelids drooped, and he could barely keep his eyes open.»
test: Why must adults not drive after taking some medicines? | *Because the medicines may cause drowsiness | Because the medicines may cause hunger | Because the medicines may cause loud music | Because the medicines may cause bright colours
test: Which word is the describing word from drowsiness? | *Drowsy | Drowned | Drowning
exam: Which sentence uses drowsiness correctly? | *«After the long train ride, she felt a wave of drowsiness.» | «After the long train ride, she was drowsiness.» | «After the long train ride, she felt a drowsinesses.»
